window.docContents[107].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Homomorphism-of-Lie-algebras/#Chapter2___Definition2___9___6":{"contents":"Definition 2.9.6. Let \\mathfrak{g}_1, \\mathfrak{g}_2 be Lie algebras. A homomorphism of Lie algebras \\varphi : \\mathfrak{g}_1 \\longrightarrow \\mathfrak{g}_2 is a linear map such that \\varphi([a, b]) = [\\varphi(a), \\varphi(b)] for all a, b \\in \\mathfrak{g}_1.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.9. Lie algebras\tHomomorphism of Lie algebras","header":"Homomorphism of Lie algebras","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Homomorphism-of-Lie-algebras/#Chapter2___Definition2___9___6"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/#chapter-05___section-5-25":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results","header":"5.25. Representations of $GL_2(\\mathbb{F}_q)$","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/#chapter-05___section-5-25"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___3___-Principal-series/#Chapter5___Discussion_5___25___3":{"contents":"5.25.3. Principal series representations. Let\n\nB \\subset G, \\quad B = \\left\\{ \\begin{pmatrix} * & * \\\\ 0 & * \\end{pmatrix} \\right\\}\n\n(the set of upper triangular matrices); then\n\n|B| = (q - 1)^2 q,\n\n[B, B] = U = \\left\\{ \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & * \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\right\\},\n\nand\n\nB/[B, B] \\cong \\mathbb{F}_q^\\times \\times \\mathbb{F}_q^\\times\n\n(the isomorphism maps an element of B to its two diagonal entries). Let\n\n\\lambda : B \\to \\mathbb{C}^\\times\nbe a homomorphism defined by\n\n\\lambda\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ 0 & c \\end{pmatrix} = \\lambda_1(a)\\lambda_2(c)\n\nfor some pair of homomorphisms \\lambda_1, \\lambda_2 : \\mathbb{F}_q^\\times \\to \\mathbb{C}^\\times. Define\n\nV_{\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2} = \\operatorname{Ind}_B^G \\mathbb{C}_\\lambda,\n\nwhere \\mathbb{C}_\\lambda is the 1-dimensional representation of B in which B acts by \\lambda. We have\n\n\\dim(V_{\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2}) = \\frac{|G|}{|B|} = q + 1.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.25. Representations of $GL_2(\\mathbb{F}_q)$\t5.25.3. Principal series\tSection 5.25.3: Principal series representations — setup of B, V_{lambda_1,lambda_2}","header":"Section 5.25.3: Principal series representations — setup of B, V_{lambda_1,lambda_2}","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___3___-Principal-series/#Chapter5___Discussion_5___25___3"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Proof-of-Artin___s-theorem-_LPAR_both-directions_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___26___1":{"contents":"Proof. Proof that (ii) implies (i). Assume that g \\in G does not belong to any of the subgroups H \\in X. Then, since X is conjugation invariant, it cannot be conjugated into such a subgroup. Hence by the Frobenius formula, \\chi_{\\operatorname{Ind}_H^G(V)}(g) = 0 for all H \\in X and V. So by (ii), for any irreducible representation W of G, \\chi_W(g) = 0. But irreducible characters span the space of class functions, so any class function vanishes on g, which is a contradiction.\n\nProof that (i) implies (ii). Let U be a virtual representation of G over \\mathbb{C} (i.e., a linear combination of irreducible representations with nonzero integer coefficients) such that (\\chi_U, \\chi_{\\operatorname{Ind}_H^G V}) = 0 for all H, V. So by Frobenius reciprocity, (\\chi_{U|_H}, \\chi_V) = 0. This means that \\chi_U vanishes on H for any H \\in X. Hence by (i), \\chi_U is identically zero. This implies (ii) (because of Remark 5.26.2). \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.26. Artin's theorem\tProof of Artin's theorem (both directions)","header":"Proof of Artin's theorem (both directions)","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Proof-of-Artin___s-theorem-_LPAR_both-directions_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___26___1"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/#chapter-09___section-9-4":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t9. Structure of finite dimensional algebras","header":"9.4. Homological dimension","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/#chapter-09___section-9-4"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Section-9___7___-Morita-equivalence/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___7___heading-1":{"contents":"In this section we will discuss the theory of Morita equivalence of finite dimensional algebras. We note that this theory extends with appropriate changes to the infinite dimensional case.\n\nTheorem 9.6.4 shows that a finite abelian category \\mathcal{C} can be identified with the category of finite dimensional modules over a finite dimensional algebra B, once we choose a projective generator P of \\mathcal{C}. However, the projective generator is not unique. Namely, let P_1, \\ldots, P_m be the indecomposable projective objects of \\mathcal{C} (they make sense by Theorem 9.6.4). Then all the projective generators of \\mathcal{C} are the objects of the form P_{\\mathbf{n}} := \\bigoplus_{i=1}^m n_i P_i, where n_i \\geq 1 (check it!). Defining B_{\\mathbf{n}} = B_{\\mathbf{n}}(\\mathcal{C}) := \\operatorname{End}(P_{\\mathbf{n}})^{\\mathrm{op}}, we see that the algebras B_{\\mathbf{n}} are all the finite dimensional algebras whose category of finite dimensional modules is equivalent to \\mathcal{C}.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t9. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\t9.7. Morita equivalence\tSection 9.7: Morita equivalence","header":"9.7. Morita equivalence","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Section-9___7___-Morita-equivalence/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___7___heading-1"}});
